   445	#### 4.2.1 Go version literals and canonical comparison
   446	
   447	The `go` entry is complete, so its classifiers are exhaustive here and are not
   448	deferred to a driver decision. Both Go directives are compared through one
   449	literal grammar:
   450	
   451	```text
   452	goVersionLiteral = ^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)(\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*))?(\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*))?([a-z]+[0-9]+)?$
   453	```
   454	
   455	A matching literal yields a **base triple** — absent minor and patch components
   456	are `0` — and a prerelease flag set by the trailing `[a-z]+[0-9]+` group.
   457	Comparison against the resolved toolchain uses the base triple only, under the
   458	canonical order of section 2.1.
   459	
   460	Using the base triple is exact rather than approximate, which matters because
   461	Go's own order is `1.21 < 1.21rc1 < 1.21.0`: a bare language version sorts
   462	*below* its own release candidates. Both canonicalizations — language version
   463	`1.21` to `(1, 21, 0)` and prerelease `1.21rc1` to `(1, 21, 0)` — can differ
   464	from Go's order only for a comparand strictly between `1.21` and `1.21.0`, and
   465	every such value is itself a prerelease. The resolved toolchain is never a
   466	prerelease, because Stage A step 4 rejected one before Stage B ran. The two
   467	orders therefore agree on every comparison Stage B can actually perform.
   468	
   469	Curator's literal grammar is a small superset of what Go's own parser accepts:
   470	Go requires at least `major.minor` in the `go` directive and a `go1`-prefixed
   471	toolchain name. The superset is deliberate and harmless — these values are
   472	assertions, never selectors, so a wider grammar can only produce a *comparison*,
   473	never a candidate toolchain. Curator's classifier is not a substitute for the
   474	ecosystem's parser: a `go.mod` that Go itself rejects still fails in the
   475	compiler, and Stage B never converts a Go-invalid file into a Curator-valid
   476	build.
   477	
   478	Upstream grammar sources for the two classifiers below: `go.dev/ref/mod`
   479	(`go` and `toolchain` directive grammar), `go.dev/doc/toolchain` (`default` and
   480	named toolchains), and the vendored `golang.org/x/mod/modfile` `GoVersionRE`
   481	and `ToolchainRE` together with `cmd/go/internal/gover.FromToolchain`, which
   482	strips a custom suffix introduced by `-`, space, or tab and rejects a name
   483	containing a path separator.
   484	
   485	#### 4.2.2 Classifier — `go.mod` `go` directive
   486	
   487	Ordered, first match wins, total:
   488	
   489	| # | Class | Match | Disposition | Outcome |
   490	|---|---|---|---|---|
   491	| 1 | absent | the directive is not present | — | no assertion; permitted |
   492	| 2 | release literal | matches `goVersionLiteral` with no prerelease group | `compared` | base triple above resolved → `build_toolchain_metadata_mismatch`; at or below → permitted |
   493	| 3 | prerelease literal | matches `goVersionLiteral` with a prerelease group | `compared` | same base-triple comparison as class 2 |
   494	| 4 | unclassifiable | anything else | `compared` | `build_toolchain_metadata_mismatch` |
   495	
   496	The `go` directive has no `forbidden` class. Its Go-defined value space is a
   497	version and nothing else, so no value of it names *where* a toolchain comes
   498	from — a value containing a path separator is not a location under any Go
   499	reading of this directive, and lands in class 4 as unclassifiable, never as
   500	package influence. Stating this fixes the outcome instead of leaving it to an
   501	implementation's analogy with the `toolchain` directive.
   502	
   503	#### 4.2.3 Classifier — `go.mod` `toolchain` directive
   504	
   505	Let `rest` be the value with a leading `go` removed, `v` the maximal prefix of
   506	`rest` containing no `-`, space, or tab, and `suffix` the remainder. This is
   507	upstream's own split.
   508	
   509	Ordered, first match wins, total:
   510	
   511	| # | Class | Match | Disposition | Outcome |
   512	|---|---|---|---|---|
   513	| 1 | absent | the directive is not present | — | no assertion; permitted |
   514	| 2 | path-bearing name | the value contains `/` or `\` | `forbidden` | `build_toolchain_package_influence_forbidden` |
   515	| 3 | custom-distribution name | the value begins with `go` and `suffix` is non-empty (`go1.23.4-bigcorp`) | `forbidden` | `build_toolchain_package_influence_forbidden` |
   516	| 4 | `default` | the value is exactly `default` | `compared` | permitted and never honored |
   517	| 5 | release name | the value begins with `go` and `v` matches `goVersionLiteral` with no prerelease group | `compared` | base triple above resolved → `build_toolchain_metadata_mismatch`; at or below → permitted and never honored |
   518	| 6 | prerelease name | as class 5, with a prerelease group (`go1.24rc1`) | `compared` | same base-triple comparison as class 5 |
   519	| 7 | unclassifiable | anything else, including a value with no `go` prefix and an empty or malformed `v` | `compared` | `build_toolchain_metadata_mismatch` |
