   160	### 4. Two-stage preflight
   161	
   162	Stage A — availability, version, platform — runs immediately after manifest
   163	parsing and build-command validation, for every distinct toolchain in the plan,
   164	in Unicode-scalar lexical order of toolchain identifier, once per operation. It
   165	reads no package byte beyond the already-validated manifest and it MUST
   166	complete before external repository acquisition, before build-cache lookup, and
   167	before any persistent mutation.
   168	
   169	Stage B — source-metadata cross-check — runs per active build command after
   170	local snapshot validation, or after exact external acquisition and audit, and
   171	before the manager reads an artifact-cache candidate or starts a compiler
   172	child. It checks `go.mod`, `Cargo.toml` and `rust-toolchain.toml`,
   173	`swift-tools-version` and `.swift-version`, or the single Kotlin metadata field
   174	that `TASK-260728-168smo` selects.
   175	
   176	Stage B reads declared metadata **as an assertion about the already-resolved
   177	toolchain, never as a selector**. It cannot switch, download, activate, or
   178	re-resolve anything. A `rust-toolchain.toml` `channel` is `compared` and then
   179	discarded — a version literal or `stable` is permitted and never honored, while
   180	`beta`, `nightly`, and dated channels are a mismatch because they assert a
   181	prerelease host v1 never resolves. Its `components`, `targets`, and `profile`
   182	are `ignored`; its `path` is `forbidden`, and being forbidden it is evaluated
   183	first, so a file carrying both `path` and `nightly` is deterministically a
   184	package-influence rejection.
   185	
   186	Because the `go` entry is complete, its two directives are classified
   187	exhaustively here rather than deferred. The `go` directive has three value
   188	classes — release literal, prerelease literal, unclassifiable — and no
   189	`forbidden` class, because its Go-defined value space is a version and nothing
   190	else. The `toolchain` directive has six: a path-bearing name and a
   191	custom-distribution name (`go1.23.4-bigcorp`) are `forbidden`, because a vendor
   192	suffix names *where* a toolchain comes from and is the `go.mod` analogue of
   193	`rust-toolchain.toml` `path`; `default` is permitted and never honored, since it
   194	means "do not switch", which is unconditionally what Curator does; canonical
   195	release and prerelease names compare by base triple; and a catch-all is a
   196	mismatch. Comparison canonicalizes `1.23` and `1.23rc1` alike to `(1, 23, 0)`,
   197	which is exact rather than approximate: the two orders can differ only for a
   198	comparand strictly between a language version and its release, every such value
   199	is a prerelease, and Stage A already rejected a prerelease host.
   200	
   201	Neither stage may be skipped by a cache hit, a dry run, or an offline mode, and
   202	neither may be reordered ahead of source validation or audit. Stage A moving
   203	earlier does not weaken any gate: it consumes manager and operator
   204	configuration plus one already-validated manifest field, and every existing
   205	phase keeps its existing position and its existing predecessor set.
